.TH GIT-WORKFLOW-BRANCH 1 2026-10-03 git-workflow "git-workflow manual"
.SH NAME
git-workflow-branch \- start, prerelease, finish and delete branches following the flow
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B git workflow branch
.RI [ git-branch-options ]
.br
.B git workflow branch start
.BR topic | release | hotfix
.RI [ name ]
.br
.B git workflow branch pre
.RI [ label | tag ]
.RB [ \-n ]
.RB [ \-l ]
.br
.B git workflow branch finish
.RI [ branch ]
.br
.B git workflow branch delete
.RI [ branch ]
.RB [ \-y ]
.RB [ \-f ]
.br
.B git workflow branch config
|
.B help
|
.B version
.SH DESCRIPTION
With no subcommand, or one it doesn't recognize,
.B git workflow branch
runs
.BR git\-branch (1)
with the same arguments.
.PP
With the default aliases
.RB ( "git workflow aliases install" )
this is also
.BR "git b" ;
the 1.x name
.B b
works after
.B git workflow
too.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B start topic | release | hotfix \fR[\fIname\fR]
Create a branch from the right base and push it.
Topic branches start from the dev branch;
.I release/vX.Y.Z
from the dev branch;
.I hotfix/vX.Y.Z
from the main branch.
Release and hotfix versions are checked against existing tags.
Tracked edits come along, as with
.BR "git switch \-c" .
A name that already exists locally or on the remote is refused.
.TP
.B pre \fR[\fIlabel\fR | \fItag\fR] [\fB\-n\fR] [\fB\-l\fR]
Tag the current release or hotfix branch as a prerelease and push the tag.
With no argument the tag is
.IR vX.Y.Z\-rc.N ,
numbered after the existing ones; a
.I label
replaces
.BR rc ;
a full
.I tag
is used as given.
Unpushed branch commits are pushed first (after asking); a branch that is
behind its remote is refused.
.B \-n
shows what would be tagged and never prompts or stops.
.B \-l
lists the prereleases for the branch's version.
.TP
.B finish \fR[\fIbranch\fR]
A topic branch is merged into the dev branch with
.BR \-\-no\-ff ,
pushed, and deleted (or, with
.BR "workflow.finishTopic=pr" ,
pushed and opened as a pull request).
A release or hotfix branch is merged into the main branch, tagged
.IR vX.Y.Z ,
merged back into the dev branch (git flow only), and pushed with
.B git push \-\-atomic
together with that one tag.
Nothing is pushed until every local step has succeeded. If a merge
conflicts, the dev branch has diverged, or the push is rejected, finish
restores the main and dev branches, deletes the tag it made, and returns
to the branch being finished, so it can simply be run again. For a
rejected release push or a back-merge conflict it asks first, and
otherwise prints the command that completes the release by hand.
.TP
.B delete \fR[\fIbranch\fR] [\fB\-y\fR] [\fB\-f\fR]
Delete a branch locally and on the remote.
.B \-y
skips the question, and only applies when the branch is named.
A branch with commits that aren't merged into the main or dev branch
(locally or on the remote copy) is refused unless
.B \-f
is given. Protected branches are never deleted.
.TP
.B config
Show the effective settings.
.TP
.B version
Show the installed version.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
git workflow branch start release v1.2.0
git workflow branch pre              # v1.2.0\-rc.1, pushed; CI publishes a prerelease
git workflow branch pre              # v1.2.0\-rc.2
git workflow branch finish           # v1.2.0 on main, merged back into next
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR git\-workflow (1)
for configuration and guardrails.
